        <div class="api-content">
          <section id="iiif-overview" class="api-card api-card--accent">
            <h2>IIIF overview</h2>
            <p>
              The <a href="https://iiif.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">International Image Interoperability Framework</a>
              (IIIF, pronounced “triple-eye-eff”) is a set of open standards for sharing images, audiovisual files,
              and other types of digital objects online. IIIF is used by institutions such as libraries, archives,
              museums, and scientific institutes to share digital collections.
            </p>
            <p>
              One of the key benefits of IIIF is that it is open source. This means that a variety of tools and platforms,
              such as the Mirador image viewer used by Canadiana, can be built to integrate with IIIF and can be freely
              used and adapted by other institutions and developers. Additionally, because institutions using IIIF are
              employing a collective standard to display digital objects, these digital objects can easily be shared
              between different platforms, different collections, and even different institutions.
            </p>
            <p>
              IIIF began in 2011 as a collaboration between Bibliothèque nationale de France, the Bodleian Libraries,
              the British Library, Cornell University, Los Alamos National Laboratory Research Library,
              Nasjonalbiblioteket (National Library of Norway), and Stanford University. In 2015, the
              <a href="https://iiif.io/community/consortium/members/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IIIF Consortium</a>
              was founded and now includes members from across the globe. CRKN has been an
              <a href="https://www.crkn-rcdr.ca/en/crkn-joins-iiif-consortium-associate-member" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Associate Member of IIIF</a>
              since 2024.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="iiif-tools" class="api-card">
            <h2>What IIIF tools are available on Canadiana?</h2>
            <p>
              If you only need to view content on Canadiana, you can simply search and browse the collection as you
              normally would. However, Canadiana also offers several IIIF-enabled tools in the Toolbox section
              underneath the image viewer to enhance your research.
            </p>
            <ul class="api-list api-list--bulleted">
              <li>
                <strong>Copy Manifest Link:</strong> If you would like to use Canadiana content in other IIIF applications,
                you will need to copy the manifest of that item first. A manifest is an excerpt of JSON code containing
                all the information of the object, including its content and how it is structured, as well as associated
                metadata.
              </li>
              <li class="iiif-page-list-text iiif-page-list-text--manifest">
                On Canadiana, a link to the IIIF manifest for any item can be found in the Toolbox section:
                Click “Copy Manifest Link” and paste the link into the manifest URL field in any IIIF application. The
                application will then be able to read the manifest and display the item.
              </li>
              <li class="iiif-page-list-image">
                <img class="iiif-page-figure__image" src="/assets/iiif-copy-en.png" alt="The copy manifest link button can be found towards the bottom of the page." loading="lazy">
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Crop Image:</strong> This tool allows you crop and save images from Canadiana items. Click
                “Crop Image,” then use the pencil icon to draw a rectangle around the part of the image you wish to crop.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Generate PDF:</strong> The PDiiif tool allows you to generate custom PDFs from Canadiana items.
                After clicking “Generate PDF,” use the green settings icon to adjust the image size, page range, and
                optimization settings.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Edit Copy of Manifest:</strong> This tool allows you to edit a copy of a Canadiana item’s manifest.
                Use the various features to annotate images, reorder and delete pages, add images from other resources,
                and more.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section id="iiif-other-tools" class="api-card">
            <h2>What other IIIF tools are available to use?</h2>
            <p>
              There are a wide variety of external IIIF applications available that allow you to view and compare images,
              build exhibitions, annotate documents, and more. Visit the IIIF website for a list of
              <a href="https://iiif.io/get-started/tools/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">popular IIIF tools</a>
              including image and A/V viewers, digital exhibit platforms, annotation platforms, and manifest editors,
              as well as useful
              <a href="https://iiif.io/get-started/training/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IIIF training resources</a>.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="iiif-collections" class="api-card">
            <h2>Where can I find other IIIF-enabled collections?</h2>
            <p>
              Institutions around the world use IIIF for their digital collections. IIIF maintains a partial list of
              <a href="https://iiif.io/guides/finding_resources/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">participating institutions</a>,
              and you can also keep an eye out for the IIIF logo when viewing material on digital collections websites.
              Additionally, the
              <a href="https://seige.digital/en/detektiiif/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DetektIIIF browser extension</a>
              is a downloadable tool that automatically detects IIIF resources in websites.
            </p>
            <p>
              CRKN has begun a list, maintained on GitHub, of
              <a href="https://github.com/crkn-rcdr/iiif-canada-directory?tab=readme-ov-file" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Canadian institutions using IIIF</a>.
              Do you know of an institution with IIIF-enabled collections, services, or tools that’s not listed here?
              Contact us via the instructions at our GitHub page, or email <a href="mailto:info@crkn.ca">info@crkn.ca</a>
              to let us know.
            </p>
          </section>

